WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.400
Know your beads, strings and tools before you start.

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:10.810
This is the video for module one of Bead Making and Simple Crafts: materials, tools and a safe workspace.

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.180
To make pieces people will buy, first understand the beads themselves.

4
00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:21.910
You will learn to count how many beads a piece needs, so you do not run out halfway.

5
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.400
Most broken beadwork fails at the string or the clasp, not at the beads.

6
00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.660
And you will set up a safe workspace.

7
00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:35.640
Most beads are measured in millimetres across the widest part.

8
00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:43.490
A 4 mm bead is about the size of a peppercorn. An 8 mm bead is about the size of a chickpea.

9
00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:54.210
Seed beads, the tiny ones, use the aught size, written like 11/0. Say it eleven aught. Here the bigger the number, the smaller the bead.

10
00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:08.830
Many round beads come on a strand of about 40 cm. To guess the count, divide 400 by the bead size in mm. So about 100 beads of 4 mm, or 50 beads of 8 mm.

11
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.960
Step one. Decide the finished length of the piece.

12
00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:20.530
Step two. Take away the length of the clasp, jump ring and crimps, because beads do not fill that part.

13
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.260
Step three. Divide what is left by the size of one bead.

14
00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:30.520
Step four. Add about 10% spare for faulty or chipped beads.

15
00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:39.480
And before you buy, check that your wire or thread can pass through the hole twice. Carry a short piece of your wire to the shop and test a bead.

16
00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:50.530
Let us follow Halima in Kano. She wants an 18-inch necklace, about 45 cm, of 6 mm red glass beads with a lobster clasp.

17
00:01:51.780 --> 00:02:02.190
45 cm is 450 mm. The clasp, jump ring and crimps take about 20 mm, so the beads must fill about 430 mm.

18
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.490
430 divided by 6 is about 72 beads.

19
00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:15.290
She adds about 10% spare. 72 plus 7 is 79, so she plans for 80 beads.

20
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:26.760
One 40 cm strand of 6 mm beads holds about 66 beads. So she buys two strands, and keeps the rest for matching earrings or a repair.

21
00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:38.330
Beading wire is tiny steel wires twisted together and coated in nylon. It lasts longer and holds heavy beads. You finish it with crimps and crimping pliers.

22
00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:46.750
More strands inside, such as 49, means a softer drape. Use the thickest wire that still passes through your beads twice.

23
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:58.130
Elastic cord is fast and cheap and needs no clasp, but it can stretch out over time. Use it for light beads, finished with a surgeon's knot and a dot of glue.

24
00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.030
Do not use sewing thread. It frays and snaps under the weight of glass beads.

25
00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:08.410
Flush cutters cut beading wire and head pins with a flat, neat end.

26
00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.460
Chain-nose pliers grip, bend, open and close jump rings.

27
00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:18.340
Round-nose pliers make loops on head pins and eye pins.

28
00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:27.910
Crimping pliers fold crimp tubes into a strong, neat shape. Never use your teeth to flatten a crimp. It gives weak work and can hurt you.

29
00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:35.000
Needles carry thread, and a bead mat stops beads rolling. A folded towel works to start.

30
00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:48.520
Beads, crimps and jump rings are choking hazards. Keep containers closed and out of reach of young children, and sweep the floor after work. Never make bead necklaces for babies or toddlers.

31
00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:58.280
Cut wire pointing down into a bag or your palm, because short ends can fly off. Wear ordinary glasses or safety glasses.

32
00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:10.200
Sit at a table, not on the floor or bed, with good light. Every 20 minutes, look at something about 20 feet away for 20 seconds, and stand up and stretch every hour.

33
00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:27.640
To remember. Start with glass and crystal-style beads. Read sizes in millimetres, and aught sizes for seed beads. Count your beads and add 10% spare. Use crimps for wire and a knot for elastic. And keep small parts away from children.

34
00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:45.150
Now practise. Make a bead reference card with five labelled beads, write your starter findings list with approximate prices, set up your workspace, and measure three wrists. For a bracelet, add 1.5 to 2.5 cm to the wrist measurement.

35
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.440
Continue with the lessons below.
